00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.450
PATRIK WESTIN: Hi, and welcome
to this breakout session

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.370
on the Cloud Identity
and Access Management.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.650
And today I'm going
to focus on how

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:21.740
to manage your cloud policies
efficiently and at scale.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:26.100
So in this session, I'm breaking
it out into four pieces.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.070
We're going to start with the
core concept of Cloud IAM, what

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.420
it means and what we do.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.080
Secondly, we're going
to have a deep dive

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.850
into a bunch of best practices.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:41.260
How do you actually
manage at scale?

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.170
Third, we have a customer story
from Credit Karma coming up

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:51.280
the stage, Matt, explaining how
they manage their resources.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.700
And last, we have
a super secret,

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.430
not yet launched feature, that
you're getting a sneak peek.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.900
So take a step back.

00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.560
And what is Cloud IAM?

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:06.610
What do we do?

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:08.800
We answer the
question who can take

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:11.870
what action on which resource.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:16.120
This other policies
in cloud which--

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.610
people sometimes get
confused and think, oh,

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:19.929
is this identity?

00:01:19.929 --> 00:01:20.470
No, it's not.

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:24.370
So org policies,
specifically, are which

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:26.560
configurations you can have.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:30.887
Another example is quotas,
which is how much money can I

00:01:30.887 --> 00:01:34.840
spend, how much have
I spent, where do we--

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.750
who pays the bill
for this resource.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.990
So the first, we're
doing a deep dive

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:50.460
into the core
concept of Cloud IAM.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:53.820
Cloud IAM provides a
fine-grained access control,

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:59.120
and we do that in
multiple dimensions.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:03.150
And some things I'm going
to talk to you today about

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.540
is appropriate if you're a
garage, you're a startup,

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.669
or you're just a happy student.

00:02:08.669 --> 00:02:13.950
Other things are focused
on large-scale deployments.

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:17.780
So we start with the
core, core concept.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:23.100
Permissions-- everything in IAM
it's based on IAM permissions.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.810
They have a special structure.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:29.970
They start with a service name
followed by the resource type,

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:36.130
and then the verb or the action
you can take on this resource.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:40.650
Let's take an example,
storage.buckets.create.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:44.640
It's obvious by the name
of these controls who

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:47.670
can create buckets.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:53.070
There's two others that are
very common, two actions.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.760
And that is get IAM
policy and set IAM policy.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:59.460
They exist for
every resource type.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:02.320
And I know I'm biased,
but in my mind,

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.590
these are the most
important permissions

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.540
because these are
the permissions that

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.130
allow you to grant more
permissions to other people

00:03:11.130 --> 00:03:12.810
or to yourself.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.930
So we'll get back to
that in later slides.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:19.380
But remember,
these are powerful.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:22.680
Of course, creating
or updating a bucket

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.730
could also be critical if
you have something secret

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.110
in that bucket.

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.750
However, the set IAM
policy, because it

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:33.780
allows you to
grant new policies,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.390
is order of magnitude
more powerful.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:41.240
So let's take it
from the beginning.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.470
An API is called.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:49.240
Say we want to call compute
engine to get an instance.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:50.410
You call it.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.720
It will be translated
from the back end

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.424
to check against IAM to see,
do you have this permission?

00:03:57.424 --> 00:03:58.840
In this specific
case, we're going

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:02.770
to check if you have the
compute instance get permission

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.080
on instance number 1.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:11.380
If you do, you will get
the data you ask for.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:15.310
If you don't, you will
get an access denied.

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:17.829
So that is a simple API.

00:04:17.829 --> 00:04:20.310
Now let's make it more fun.

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:23.510
Other APIs take a lot
of different variables.

00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.700
In this case, we are going
to one API call comes in

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.950
to compute engine,
it's going to translate

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:35.530
into four different permission
checks on different resource

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:36.730
types.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:40.710
And for this API call
to be successful,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.410
you need to have all four
permissions on those resource

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.730
types, or this API
call will fail.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:54.260
So now some of you are in the
audience think, like, hey,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.312
permissions?

00:04:55.312 --> 00:04:56.520
I don't deal with permission.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.070
I've used IAM before.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.920
I only care about the roles.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:03.450
And to some degree
you're correct.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:08.090
Roles is the abstraction we
use to grouping permissions

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:09.980
together.

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:14.690
So you can think about this
as performing certain tasks

00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:19.850
or certain workflows that
permissions belong together

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.790
because it allows a
user to accomplish

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.590
a set of tasks or workflows.

00:05:28.670 --> 00:05:33.980
So we have actually
three different types

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.910
of roles in Cloud IAM.

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:43.500
We have primitive roles,
which we have there.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.300
They're very easy to use.

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.810
We actually don't recommend
that you use this in production.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:53.330
These are very, very powerful
and it makes it very easy

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:54.540
to get started.

00:05:54.540 --> 00:05:57.500
Again, if you're a student
or are you just tinkering,

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.090
feel free to use this.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:02.480
If you're working for a large
bank or something like that,

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.270
please don't use this.

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.320
Instead, you should
use predefined roles.

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:10.490
They are narrower.

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:15.740
They group things normally
bound per service.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:17.720
And there's several,
several hundreds

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:19.850
of these predefined
roles that Google

00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:23.720
have defined that we think
that, oh, these belong together.

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:28.040
This can accomplish
certain tasks.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.460
There is a few patterns there
that, basically, every service

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:36.830
have an admin and a viewer
of a predefined role.

00:06:36.830 --> 00:06:39.710
There are many
specialized roles as well,

00:06:39.710 --> 00:06:44.900
but could also be that your
specific use case calls

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.820
for something more specialized.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.210
We call that custom roles.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:53.030
Custom roles allow you to
combine any permissions

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:58.100
together and create anything
you want, and say, for me--

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:01.030
for my use case--

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:02.920
I will have this workflow.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:07.630
And I need my users to have
this set of permissions

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:10.690
because, to me,
they go together.

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:14.290
Maybe not go together
for all of you,

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:16.750
but for your specific use
case that makes sense.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:25.460
Custom roles, while they
can be used for anything,

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.170
there's three very
common use cases.

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.790
These two combin roles, you
look at the predefined roles

00:07:31.790 --> 00:07:34.790
that we have worked, that
different teams heave

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:39.200
worked hard to create and say,
oh, I like these two roles

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.050
and I'm always granting
them at the same time.

00:07:42.050 --> 00:07:44.010
Let's combine them.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.970
Any other one is, like,
hey, I like this role,

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:49.760
but it has this powerful
permission I don't like.

00:07:49.760 --> 00:07:51.290
Let's remove it.

00:07:51.290 --> 00:07:56.330
Or this role has everything I
need but this one permission,

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:57.590
and you can add it.

00:07:57.590 --> 00:08:00.710
So these are the most
common use cases.

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:03.710
However, you can combine
anything you want.

00:08:06.530 --> 00:08:12.010
So now we talked about the
permissions and the roles.

00:08:12.010 --> 00:08:16.010
And now we need to
bind these to users.

00:08:16.010 --> 00:08:18.510
We call these bindings.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:19.870
Obvious name, right?

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:23.950
And you can bind it
to an individual user

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:26.680
or to a group of users.

00:08:26.680 --> 00:08:31.540
That group can also be a group
of a group of a group, nested,

00:08:31.540 --> 00:08:35.260
and everyone in the whole
chain of those groups

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:37.950
are going to be granted.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:42.850
The third type is
a service account

00:08:42.850 --> 00:08:46.180
and we're going to come
back to that later slides.

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:49.870
Services account is
basically an identity

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:53.200
of a service or a robot.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:09:00.760
And all of these bindings can be
combined so you, in one policy,

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:05.200
have multiple bindings with
multiple different roles

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.960
for multiple different people.

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:11.275
And then, when you combine all
of those, you have a policy.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:16.570
So for you that like JSON--

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:20.780
this is my favorite example.

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:29.210
So you can actually write this
and use command line APIs.

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:33.080
And in this specific example,
we grant the compute instance

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:37.430
admin role to two
users, to a user

00:09:37.430 --> 00:09:40.910
that is Mike and a group
that is the admins.

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:46.480
We say, this is my
powerful role and I

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:50.040
am very selective on
who I grant this to.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:55.910
I have another role that is
less powerful, compute viewer.

00:09:55.910 --> 00:09:59.030
I can grant them to
everyone in my domain

00:09:59.030 --> 00:10:04.540
or I can grant them to a
specific service account.

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:09.670
So we have now talked
about our permissions

00:10:09.670 --> 00:10:11.650
and how we can bind
into roles, how

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:15.400
we bind them to
users, our members,

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:18.310
and now we talk about hierarchy.

00:10:18.310 --> 00:10:22.600
So all of these can be
granted in hierarchy.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.190
And this is a core core to how
Google do identity and access

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:28.970
management.

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:32.080
You can grant these
policies, literally,

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:35.410
anywhere in your
resource hierarchy.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:41.050
If you start granting something
in the org level, super high

00:10:41.050 --> 00:10:44.950
up in your hierarchy, it
will be very powerful.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:48.670
The higher up, the more
powerful because the permissions

00:10:48.670 --> 00:10:50.530
is inherited down.

00:10:50.530 --> 00:10:55.570
So everyone in your org, every
resource that is ever created

00:10:55.570 --> 00:10:58.390
would be affected
by this policy.

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.740
Versus if you do
the other extreme,

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:04.840
you put a policy on the
individual resource.

00:11:04.840 --> 00:11:11.200
Now only that resource will be
granted, that specific role.

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:14.200
That is also not the best
practice because odds

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.690
are you might be
successful one day

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.970
and end up having
millions and millions

00:11:18.970 --> 00:11:21.910
and millions of these
resources, and now you

00:11:21.910 --> 00:11:25.160
have millions and millions
of policies to manage.

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:29.140
So the best practice
is to think about how

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:31.630
you in your organization work.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:33.780
What makes sense?

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:36.040
And I'm going to come
back in the best practice

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:40.540
and do a couple of deep
dives in exactly why we

00:11:40.540 --> 00:11:43.130
use the hierarchy and how we
recommend reason about it.

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:49.950
I told you I was going to
come back to service accounts.

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:55.680
So think about service account
as the identity of one service.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.570
And this is best practice
that this service account

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:02.790
should take the identity of
a specific service you have.

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:06.350
This service account can
manage and be the identity

00:12:06.350 --> 00:12:09.060
of multiple virtual machines.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.600
You can have multiple
service accounts

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.680
inside the same
project and these

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:22.320
can be granted any other role so
they can act on other projects

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.250
and on other resources.

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:29.400
So in this example here, you
see that this service account

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:32.310
acts on buckets
in other projects

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:33.660
and that's totally fine.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:37.980
And you can say one service
account access one bucket

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:41.230
and my other service account
access my other bucket.

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:47.810
And this is where people
sometimes get slightly

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:49.520
confused, I have to admit.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.460
And that is that these
service accounts,

00:12:51.460 --> 00:12:54.380
they are both an
identity by themselves

00:12:54.380 --> 00:13:01.250
and a resource, which
means that you as a user

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:04.240
can grant access to the
service account themselves.

00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:06.380
And the service
accounts is an identity.

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:12.830
So there's one powerful
permission, especially,

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:14.960
that I'm going to
come back to, as it

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:18.330
relates to service account,
that is very, very powerful.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:23.080
So just save that for now.

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:30.730
Now we're going into our second
part, the best practices.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:34.960
So say that you're
starting up and you just

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:38.720
had your first data
scientist join the company.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.140
You don't have
that many resources

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:45.460
and you want to grant her
two different roles for two

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:47.500
different resources.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:51.520
And you might be
inclined to say,

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:56.650
oh, yes, let's put Alice in the
binding for these two policies.

00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:59.200
That's not best practice.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.070
If you instead,
from day one, create

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:05.560
a group called Data
Scientist in this example

00:14:05.560 --> 00:14:11.890
and you add Alice to that group,
once your usage in Cloud grows,

00:14:11.890 --> 00:14:15.790
it might be that you add many,
many more resources and many

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:17.500
more policies.

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:21.490
And then, as you
are very successful,

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:24.310
you hire another data scientist.

00:14:24.310 --> 00:14:28.420
Now instead of finding all of
the places where I've granted

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:32.830
Alice permissions, you
just add your new hire

00:14:32.830 --> 00:14:35.800
into that Data Scientist group.

00:14:35.800 --> 00:14:39.640
And you know that now
Alice and the new hire

00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:43.040
will have the same permission
to the same resources.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:44.710
This allows you to
manage at scale.

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:53.080
In the beginning, I talked
about the super powerful set IAM

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:55.340
policy permission.

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:58.130
And as they are so
powerful because you

00:14:58.130 --> 00:15:03.080
can grant additional
policies, additional

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:07.320
permissions to other
people and to yourself--

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:11.040
remember that.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:12.200
This is very powerful.

00:15:12.200 --> 00:15:15.400
It doesn't just leave it to you
to grant other people access.

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:18.130
You can escalate your
own privilege, which

00:15:18.130 --> 00:15:20.990
makes this very, very powerful.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:26.590
So think in this hierarchy,
who do grant is permission to

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.490
and how high in the hierarchy?

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:31.660
Remember, the higher in the
hierarchy, the more resource

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:33.130
they can grant access to.

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:39.310
The other one, as I
mentioned, is the act

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:42.260
as service account
permission, which

00:15:42.260 --> 00:15:45.170
means that the user can
act and do everything

00:15:45.170 --> 00:15:47.900
as the service account can do.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:52.340
So the best practice here is
to grant this on the service

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:56.330
account themselves, and
not up in hierarchy,

00:15:56.330 --> 00:16:00.230
even though you will be
very tempted because it's

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.180
convenient at this
point in time.

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:05.060
Because later in
time, also, you're

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:07.640
going to create more
service accounts.

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:12.530
And now that grant is
automatically granting those

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:16.280
users to act as all of those
new service account that

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:18.530
didn't used to
exist but now might

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.870
have accessed on sensitive data.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:25.580
So this is the one case
where you really, really want

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:29.310
to put your policy on
the resource itself.

00:16:29.310 --> 00:16:31.542
In this case, the
resource is an identity,

00:16:31.542 --> 00:16:32.750
which is the service account.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:39.900
And we have seen some of these.

00:16:39.900 --> 00:16:41.890
You know, it's very convenient.

00:16:41.890 --> 00:16:43.920
There's an API.

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:47.220
It has a free field,
say, a description.

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.740
You can put anything in there.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:50.970
Please don't.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:54.990
If you have secrets,
treat them as secrets.

00:16:54.990 --> 00:17:00.510
Put them in a storage bucket
and put the privileges

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:04.920
and the permissions that is
appropriate for that secret

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:10.770
bucket that you have and not
try to code it in as metadata

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:12.105
in other policies.

00:17:17.450 --> 00:17:21.890
And this leads us
to leave a trace.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:26.210
Say that you just created your
super secret bucket with all

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:31.610
the company secrets, and
someone has access to it

00:17:31.610 --> 00:17:33.380
and you don't know who.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:39.730
So by default all changes,
admin changes to the cloud

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:40.770
is audit logged.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:43.940
So if you do a set IAM policy,
it's going to be logged

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:46.910
and you can see
who did that, who

00:17:46.910 --> 00:17:49.010
granted the
permission, who changed

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:51.620
the policy, that is logged.

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:54.710
But we don't know
by default that you

00:17:54.710 --> 00:17:59.030
have a super secret bucket
and you care deeply about it.

00:17:59.030 --> 00:18:01.110
So in this IAM policy.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:04.970
You can also turn
on audit login.

00:18:04.970 --> 00:18:08.210
And you can log
when someone change,

00:18:08.210 --> 00:18:13.100
in this case, this bucket
or if someone access it,

00:18:13.100 --> 00:18:14.630
like who reads this bucket.

00:18:14.630 --> 00:18:18.590
Because, remember, it's
my super secret bucket.

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:23.090
If you want to retain these logs
for a longer period of time,

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:26.930
you can export them to cloud
storage or to big query

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:28.510
if you want to query them.

00:18:28.510 --> 00:18:33.650
There's also centralized logging
systems such as Stackdriver

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:37.220
that can trigger events
and have things happen

00:18:37.220 --> 00:18:42.800
when certain logs come in.

00:18:42.800 --> 00:18:46.110
And I know, I'm biased.

00:18:46.110 --> 00:18:48.900
I hope this is big
enough for you to see.

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:55.170
Every week IAM publish on
new permissions and roles.

00:18:55.170 --> 00:19:00.150
And regardless if you use the
predefined roles or the custom

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:04.620
roles, a best practice
is to visit this page

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:08.850
and see what has
changed for two reasons.

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:12.780
If you use the
predefined roles, we

00:19:12.780 --> 00:19:14.710
will tell you that
this coming week we're

00:19:14.710 --> 00:19:19.085
going to add permission to a
role that you might be using.

00:19:19.085 --> 00:19:24.060
And you're like, oh, that's good
to know because, effectively,

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:27.420
this new permission's
being added into my system.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:31.440
The other way is you
use custom roles.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:35.880
And there's a new API or
a new argument to an API

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:38.880
that you really want to
use, but your custom rules

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:42.330
didn't have that permission
because we just made it.

00:19:42.330 --> 00:19:46.680
So you have to manually
read through it and say, ah,

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:50.790
I need to have this
permission to my custom roles

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:55.530
so that my developers or
my robots or my service

00:19:55.530 --> 00:20:01.080
can take and call this new
API or use these new arguments

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:01.970
for this API.

00:20:05.030 --> 00:20:10.250
Bring your organization
structure to the cloud,

00:20:10.250 --> 00:20:13.560
and this is just practical.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.640
It's you setting
up your company.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:20.480
You have thought
hopefully deeply about who

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:23.540
reports to whom in reporting
chains and, if something

00:20:23.540 --> 00:20:25.970
breaks, who do you go to.

00:20:25.970 --> 00:20:31.110
If your cloud resource
hierarchy doesn't match that,

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:35.360
how do you now find the person
responsible to grant you

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:36.920
more privilege?

00:20:36.920 --> 00:20:40.010
Something is broken, might
be broken in your production

00:20:40.010 --> 00:20:45.850
system, and you need,
temporarily, more access.

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:47.820
Who do you ask?

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.540
If this match how your
company is set up,

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:53.280
if that person is your
manager, that's a great thing.

00:20:57.060 --> 00:21:02.130
I promised some deep dives into
how we leverage the hierarchy.

00:21:02.130 --> 00:21:06.570
And this is the first
of the three examples.

00:21:06.570 --> 00:21:10.070
So the setup is that
we have developers

00:21:10.070 --> 00:21:17.840
and IT admins, and we have the
IT admins that have projects

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:21.150
and, in this project, they
have the shared images.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:25.100
Their role and their
work, actually,

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:28.980
is to manage these images,
make sure they're up to date,

00:21:28.980 --> 00:21:31.760
they are patched whenever
there's a security

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:35.180
incident, someone publish,
whatever operating system

00:21:35.180 --> 00:21:38.790
you're using, a new image.

00:21:38.790 --> 00:21:45.060
So you want to grant the IT
admins the instance admin role

00:21:45.060 --> 00:21:49.420
so they can change and update
all images under this project.

00:21:49.420 --> 00:21:52.090
Notice this is on
the project itself.

00:21:52.090 --> 00:21:55.350
So as images comes
and goes, you don't

00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:58.380
have to change your policies.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:00.870
It's an important detail there.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:06.360
And on the project, you also
grant the developers image user

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:11.520
roles so that all your
developers can use these images

00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:13.140
in their projects.

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:16.800
And then the developers
themself in their projects

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:19.440
will have instance
admin, and they can then

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:24.700
use the images that the IP
admins have created and managed

00:22:24.700 --> 00:22:25.200
for them.

00:22:28.690 --> 00:22:30.530
Let's take another example.

00:22:30.530 --> 00:22:32.070
We have the same setup--

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.390
developers, IT admins.

00:22:34.390 --> 00:22:37.000
In this case, however,
it's different

00:22:37.000 --> 00:22:40.060
because we have
different subnetworks.

00:22:40.060 --> 00:22:43.900
And some subnetworks
are made for developers

00:22:43.900 --> 00:22:47.920
to play and experiment
and develop.

00:22:47.920 --> 00:22:51.880
We have other where running
your sensitive workload

00:22:51.880 --> 00:22:53.450
in production.

00:22:53.450 --> 00:22:57.910
So instead of granting
the developers up

00:22:57.910 --> 00:23:01.210
on the project level,
say, use any subnetwork

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:04.810
you want, you will
grant them the policy

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:07.970
on the subnetwork
itself that you

00:23:07.970 --> 00:23:11.920
allow for them to develop on.

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:14.340
For the developer, no change.

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:18.630
They will grant the
same instance admin

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.190
on their projects.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:23.380
And whatever time they
create new projects,

00:23:23.380 --> 00:23:30.320
that can use this subnetwork
too in this example.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:33.640
Let's make the hierarchy
a little bit deeper.

00:23:33.640 --> 00:23:36.730
We have a finance team.

00:23:36.730 --> 00:23:38.800
First of all, we have a
company that is larger.

00:23:38.800 --> 00:23:40.590
We start to have divisions.

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:43.840
Someone in this division
is responsible for managing

00:23:43.840 --> 00:23:44.900
your budgets.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:47.260
How much can we spend?

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:49.020
You have subteams.

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:52.810
The different teams can
see how much they spend,

00:23:52.810 --> 00:23:56.200
but not how much the
other teams spend,

00:23:56.200 --> 00:24:00.130
and they cannot change
their own allowance.

00:24:00.130 --> 00:24:02.380
They cannot set
their own budgets.

00:24:02.380 --> 00:24:05.320
You want the finance
manage to do that.

00:24:05.320 --> 00:24:09.600
So you will grant the finance
manager for this division

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:14.260
the billing account
admin role, as high up

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:18.400
in the hierarchy as you can, in
this example, in the division.

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:20.920
And again, as high
up as you can,

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:24.430
you want to grant the
billing account viewers

00:24:24.430 --> 00:24:29.470
to the teams that are
affected, so team A and team B,

00:24:29.470 --> 00:24:34.100
you put those in and what
we call a folder level.

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:37.930
This allows any project
a team B creates

00:24:37.930 --> 00:24:42.600
in the folder to automatically
have the correct policies.

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:47.520
Instead of having to change
and remember to go back,

00:24:47.520 --> 00:24:50.820
for every project that team
B creates in the future,

00:24:50.820 --> 00:24:54.840
to add themself the permission
or the policy to see this.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:58.110
You just put it one
level up and its there.

00:25:00.930 --> 00:25:06.660
And now we are growing
the company even larger.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:10.920
And is like, oh, I'm
doing these workflows.

00:25:10.920 --> 00:25:14.940
And you will find that you
have a very common patterns

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:17.250
that, in this example,
you create the project.

00:25:17.250 --> 00:25:19.200
You might create an app in-game.

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:22.770
You want to enable billing,
you want to enable some APIs,

00:25:22.770 --> 00:25:24.715
you want to create
service accounts,

00:25:24.715 --> 00:25:27.210
and you want to
set some policies.

00:25:27.210 --> 00:25:29.940
And it's like, oh,
that's a lot of work.

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:32.910
And you might even want
to create custom roles

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:37.290
that you add to these
policies, all programmatically.

00:25:37.290 --> 00:25:41.910
Of course, you can go out
and write your own scripts

00:25:41.910 --> 00:25:44.550
and do exactly the
things you want.

00:25:44.550 --> 00:25:49.620
Or you can use the
Deployment Manager.

00:25:49.620 --> 00:25:53.040
It's a declarative tool
that allows you to focus

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:55.360
on what's important to you.

00:25:55.360 --> 00:25:58.080
What's the properties
of the virtual machine

00:25:58.080 --> 00:25:59.890
you want to configure?

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.560
You can say, I want
my virtual machine

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:03.180
to have this much memory.

00:26:03.180 --> 00:26:05.460
I want this type of CPU.

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:10.230
And you actually no longer have
to care about how this happens.

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:14.010
You go to Deployment Manager
and say, this is what I want.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.320
And you're setting
up these flows.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:18.810
And then you're done.

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:24.420
It's a fully hosted service
and it abstracts a way that--

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:27.160
it makes sense when you're
having the same workflow,

00:26:27.160 --> 00:26:29.080
creating a lot of
resources at scale.

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:35.350
And now we have storytime.

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:38.560
So Naveen from Credit
Karma is coming up

00:26:38.560 --> 00:26:45.951
to tell us about how they
leverage IAM and the policies.

00:26:45.951 --> 00:26:47.242
NAVEEN GUDDATI: Thanks, Patrik.

00:26:50.730 --> 00:26:53.130
Hello, everyone.

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:55.460
My name Naveen Guddati,
working for Credit Karma

00:26:55.460 --> 00:26:57.860
as a senior security engineer.

00:26:57.860 --> 00:27:01.040
So today I'm here to
share the Credit Karma

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:04.760
story of implementing identity
access management in the Google

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:06.650
Cloud Platform.

00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:08.370
So before we jump
into the details,

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:10.310
let me just give you
a brief background

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:12.440
on what we do at Credit Karma.

00:27:12.440 --> 00:27:14.630
So we actually offer a
lot of financial services

00:27:14.630 --> 00:27:17.930
to our members like
credit cards, auto loans,

00:27:17.930 --> 00:27:20.990
personal loans,
recommendations, et cetera.

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:23.180
So our core mission
at Credit Karma

00:27:23.180 --> 00:27:26.360
is to help our members make
financial progress by utilizing

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:28.380
all these services.

00:27:28.380 --> 00:27:31.640
We have about 80 million users
who subscribe to Credit Karma

00:27:31.640 --> 00:27:33.480
and using the services.

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:36.740
So it is an absolute
requirement for us

00:27:36.740 --> 00:27:39.140
to host these services
on a platform that

00:27:39.140 --> 00:27:42.960
is much scalable,
efficient, and secure.

00:27:42.960 --> 00:27:45.890
So when we started thinking of
migrating to the Google Cloud

00:27:45.890 --> 00:27:48.710
Platform, we started off
with some initial goals

00:27:48.710 --> 00:27:54.240
that will help secure our
infrastructure in a better way.

00:27:54.240 --> 00:27:56.750
We wanted to restrict the
access to the JCP console

00:27:56.750 --> 00:27:59.260
so that we would allow
only a specific set of one

00:27:59.260 --> 00:28:03.170
team or individuals accessing
this JCP environment.

00:28:03.170 --> 00:28:05.000
We also wanted enable
this [INAUDIBLE]

00:28:05.000 --> 00:28:07.610
single sign-on process
so that our employees can

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.590
use their own
enterprise credentials

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:12.880
and doesn't need to use a
different ID or anything.

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:14.630
And at the same time,
we wanted to enforce

00:28:14.630 --> 00:28:16.940
these multi-factor
authentication checks

00:28:16.940 --> 00:28:20.120
and perform the authorization by
implementing the JCP role that

00:28:20.120 --> 00:28:22.340
has access controls.

00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:24.980
And we would like to reduce the
burden on our administrators

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:28.070
without manually approaching
and introducing the JCP platform

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:32.110
and just by automating it, and
minimize the human activity

00:28:32.110 --> 00:28:34.950
in the JCP environment by
leveraging the JCP automation

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:36.320
model.

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:38.466
So how do we do this?

00:28:38.466 --> 00:28:41.360
The first step of the
process is authentication.

00:28:41.360 --> 00:28:44.630
We know that JCP is powered
by the cloud identities that

00:28:44.630 --> 00:28:46.740
can be created via the G Suite.

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:48.740
So what we did was we
procured the G Suite

00:28:48.740 --> 00:28:52.080
and we hosted all of
our identities in there.

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:55.510
So G Suite also supports the
single sign-on capabilities

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:57.950
that can be enabled via SAML.

00:28:57.950 --> 00:29:01.370
So we took advantage of that
having this SSO enabled,

00:29:01.370 --> 00:29:04.650
we were able to delegate all of
our authentication activities

00:29:04.650 --> 00:29:08.540
to our own [INAUDIBLE]
IDP platform.

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:10.910
And that also gave
us the flexibility

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:13.490
where you can enforce additional
security restrictions,

00:29:13.490 --> 00:29:17.090
like enforcing MFA
and also making sure

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:19.970
that users are coming from
the appropriate networks

00:29:19.970 --> 00:29:23.030
while accessing
these JCP resources.

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:26.130
Additionally, as a
best security practice,

00:29:26.130 --> 00:29:28.520
we wanted to enforce the
limits on the session

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:30.020
length of the users.

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:32.840
And G Suite has the
flexibility for that.

00:29:32.840 --> 00:29:35.960
So if a user exits a
particular configured limit,

00:29:35.960 --> 00:29:38.270
they will be forced to
reauthenticate and identify

00:29:38.270 --> 00:29:40.020
themselves.

00:29:40.020 --> 00:29:42.260
So having this
business in place,

00:29:42.260 --> 00:29:44.480
we were able to secure
the path of accessing

00:29:44.480 --> 00:29:47.660
the JCP for the employees.

00:29:47.660 --> 00:29:50.780
Now let's take a look
at how this flow works.

00:29:50.780 --> 00:29:52.310
Employees on the
Credit Karma side

00:29:52.310 --> 00:29:55.660
will be accessing the
IDP portal and they

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:58.940
will be authenticating to this
IDP portal with the credentials

00:29:58.940 --> 00:30:03.830
that [INAUDIBLE] services, which
is also hosted in our premises.

00:30:03.830 --> 00:30:06.800
Once the user is successfully
authenticated to the portal,

00:30:06.800 --> 00:30:09.290
our platform is going
to generate a SAML token

00:30:09.290 --> 00:30:12.020
and force it onto the G Suite.

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:15.620
Now, here this G Suite, which
is acting as a SAML service

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:17.660
provider, verifies
affects the authenticity

00:30:17.660 --> 00:30:20.330
of the token by
verifying the certificate

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:22.610
signature and
identity [INAUDIBLE]..

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:24.680
And if everything is
well, then G Suite

00:30:24.680 --> 00:30:27.860
is done redirecting the user
to the Google Cloud Platform

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:29.990
by using this [INAUDIBLE].

00:30:29.990 --> 00:30:33.570
So this way, we were able
to secure the path of access

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:35.450
into Google Cloud
Platform, making

00:30:35.450 --> 00:30:38.280
sure the user is going through
all the security checks

00:30:38.280 --> 00:30:39.530
that we enforce along the way.

00:30:42.400 --> 00:30:46.080
Now you have the authenticated
user on the JCP platform

00:30:46.080 --> 00:30:48.720
and the next part is
the authorization.

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:51.100
How do we do this?

00:30:51.100 --> 00:30:55.530
So in JCP, when we are trying
to authorize the users, first

00:30:55.530 --> 00:31:00.840
of all, we would like to enforce
the concept of least privilege,

00:31:00.840 --> 00:31:04.447
which is by using the predefined
roles or the custom roles.

00:31:04.447 --> 00:31:06.530
But when do we use these
predefined roles and when

00:31:06.530 --> 00:31:08.090
do we use these custom roles?

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:10.920
Let's take an example.

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:14.160
If a developer is
requesting an access

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:16.600
to one of the JCP resources
like the storage bucket,

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:19.770
as Patrik mentioned, we
will be assigning them

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:22.830
with three different roles
like our storage bucket

00:31:22.830 --> 00:31:26.370
owner or the writer,
or even the admin role

00:31:26.370 --> 00:31:28.980
so that the developer can get
all the permissions that he

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:33.420
need where he can update objects
inside the bucket or delete

00:31:33.420 --> 00:31:35.050
objects inside the bucket.

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:37.380
But these predefined
roles at sometimes

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:39.110
gives you an
additional privileges

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:40.924
of setting the IAM
policies, which

00:31:40.924 --> 00:31:43.340
means where he can delegate
the access to some other users

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:44.440
as well.

00:31:44.440 --> 00:31:47.580
And we don't want it to
do that because now we

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:51.810
want it to enforce the concept
of segregation of duties

00:31:51.810 --> 00:31:54.750
because we have a separate
team who is actually

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:57.240
performing that access
management activity.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:01.300
And this developer was supposed
to only work on the bucket

00:32:01.300 --> 00:32:02.930
by updating objects
inside the bucket

00:32:02.930 --> 00:32:05.340
or deleting or creating
the new buckets.

00:32:05.340 --> 00:32:08.520
So this is where we started
creating the custom roles.

00:32:08.520 --> 00:32:10.770
And creating the custom roles
is very easy in the JCP.

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:13.920
You can just create it
from an existing role

00:32:13.920 --> 00:32:16.470
by unchecking the permissions
that you don't need.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.600
Or even you can
create a custom role

00:32:18.600 --> 00:32:22.290
by selecting each and
every granular permission

00:32:22.290 --> 00:32:24.460
that you need.

00:32:24.460 --> 00:32:26.760
And once we have
this exercise done

00:32:26.760 --> 00:32:28.740
of granularizing
the existing roles

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:31.370
or creating all the
required custom rules,

00:32:31.370 --> 00:32:34.200
then we started off with
creating the Google Groups.

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:36.470
And as a best
practice, we always

00:32:36.470 --> 00:32:39.300
map our roles to
the Google Groups

00:32:39.300 --> 00:32:44.681
so that we don't need to manage
this access activity at the JCP

00:32:44.681 --> 00:32:45.180
IAM.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:48.610
We don't need to change
these policies every time.

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:51.330
And we can do that actually
at the Google Groups.

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:54.120
And we always try to
avoid assigning these JCP

00:32:54.120 --> 00:32:58.500
roles to the individuals
in the JCP IAM platform.

00:32:58.500 --> 00:33:02.610
And then granting privileges
only an as needed basis.

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:05.420
This is to make
sure that when we

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:07.620
are trying to elevate the
privileges of a developer

00:33:07.620 --> 00:33:09.780
for a specific
direction, we don't

00:33:09.780 --> 00:33:12.330
want to keep these privileges
on a forever basis.

00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:16.670
We wanted to revoke them once
the developer's job is done.

00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:19.508
And this is a concept where
we implement this privilege

00:33:19.508 --> 00:33:21.180
bracketing.

00:33:21.180 --> 00:33:24.150
And the next thing is
auditing and monitoring.

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:27.000
We use the tools
like [INAUDIBLE]

00:33:27.000 --> 00:33:29.640
which continuously keep
track of all the policy

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:33.120
changes happening on
the JCP IAM and also

00:33:33.120 --> 00:33:35.400
notify us with any
violations happening

00:33:35.400 --> 00:33:38.480
there so that we can go back
in there and then fix that.

00:33:41.080 --> 00:33:44.050
This way, again,
implementing these concepts

00:33:44.050 --> 00:33:47.250
and having this the role best
access controls in place,

00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:49.570
we were able to address
the users who were

00:33:49.570 --> 00:33:52.970
accessing the JCP resources.

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:55.380
And finally, the
JCP automations.

00:33:55.380 --> 00:33:58.960
We use these JCP automations
to effectively manage

00:33:58.960 --> 00:34:01.640
our infrastructure
resources and projects.

00:34:01.640 --> 00:34:04.340
Again, we follow the best
practices recommended by Google

00:34:04.340 --> 00:34:06.640
while implementing
these automations.

00:34:06.640 --> 00:34:09.070
We started off with creating
an apparent hard node

00:34:09.070 --> 00:34:12.040
at the top which
represents our enterprise.

00:34:12.040 --> 00:34:15.730
And then each and every
department in our enterprise

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:18.699
is represented by
using the JCP folders.

00:34:18.699 --> 00:34:20.800
Once you get into
the department,

00:34:20.800 --> 00:34:25.060
as department own multiple
applications and the product,

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:28.480
we created a separate project
but each and every application

00:34:28.480 --> 00:34:30.639
that we hosted in there.

00:34:30.639 --> 00:34:32.980
And finally, each
and every product

00:34:32.980 --> 00:34:38.389
is assigned with all its
required JCP resources.

00:34:38.389 --> 00:34:41.590
So just to sum it up, we
followed these best practices

00:34:41.590 --> 00:34:44.400
where we delegate the
authentication [INAUDIBLE]

00:34:44.400 --> 00:34:46.210
to our IDP platform.

00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:48.400
We used the predefined
roles or the custom

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:51.790
rules most of the time and avoid
using the [INAUDIBLE] roles.

00:34:51.790 --> 00:34:55.360
And we always map these
roles to the Google Groups

00:34:55.360 --> 00:34:58.690
and then enforce the concept
of segregation of duties.

00:34:58.690 --> 00:35:00.460
And all of these
things together will

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:02.620
help you achieve the
least [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:02.620 --> 00:35:05.320
in the JCP environment.

00:35:05.320 --> 00:35:08.330
So Patrik is going to share a
few more interesting updates

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:09.370
in the next slides.

00:35:09.370 --> 00:35:11.401
Thank you so much.

00:35:11.401 --> 00:35:13.309
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:16.650 --> 00:35:19.200
PATRIK WESTIN: So I promised
a sneak peek of something

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:21.000
that we haven't announced yet.

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:23.400
You're first to hear
it in this room--

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:25.030
IAM Conditions.

00:35:25.030 --> 00:35:27.250
And we have the some
of the developers

00:35:27.250 --> 00:35:31.090
who built it right here,
so a shout-out to them.

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:33.690
So what is IAM Conditions?

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:38.460
It's a new feature that
allows you to use information

00:35:38.460 --> 00:35:43.130
from the client, such as
what IP address was used,

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:46.330
and say, oh, if you're coming
from this IP address range,

00:35:46.330 --> 00:35:49.560
you can do more powerful things.

00:35:49.560 --> 00:35:54.990
You can-- say, if the resource
has a specific pattern,

00:35:54.990 --> 00:35:58.780
like if it starts with a
specific few characters, say,

00:35:58.780 --> 00:36:03.720
dev or prod, they're different.

00:36:03.720 --> 00:36:07.080
And you can combine
multiple of these attributes

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:10.020
with Boolean expressions
and combine it

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:12.610
into very elaborate things.

00:36:12.610 --> 00:36:16.330
So we're going to dig into
some of those examples.

00:36:16.330 --> 00:36:21.870
So say that you have
a trusted device

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:24.270
over your corporate network.

00:36:24.270 --> 00:36:29.160
And you can now grant a policy
to the editor role that is

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:32.940
powerful and say, I trust you.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:34.630
Same user.

00:36:34.630 --> 00:36:38.830
But if you're working
from your desk,

00:36:38.830 --> 00:36:42.440
you can edit and
manage this resource.

00:36:42.440 --> 00:36:46.080
But the same machine,
but you take it home.

00:36:46.080 --> 00:36:48.450
Maybe you work from home.

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:54.220
It's still your trusted device
that the company gave you.

00:36:54.220 --> 00:36:56.700
You now get the viewer role.

00:36:56.700 --> 00:37:01.610
You can only view this resource.

00:37:01.610 --> 00:37:04.260
And if you're going
to an internet cafe,

00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:05.480
you still log in.

00:37:05.480 --> 00:37:08.760
It's still you, same
correct credentials,

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:13.080
and you will not get
any access at all.

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:16.440
Another one that people
are asking for a lot

00:37:16.440 --> 00:37:19.410
is expiring policies.

00:37:19.410 --> 00:37:24.990
So you can create a policy
that has a time bond.

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:31.000
It starts at some point and
it ends at a different point.

00:37:31.000 --> 00:37:34.360
So I said that this is
a Boolean expression.

00:37:34.360 --> 00:37:39.520
You can express this in
common expression language.

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:45.430
It's a relatively new language
that Google have introduced.

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:48.992
It optimized to make it
easier to understand,

00:37:48.992 --> 00:37:51.750
to write and reason
about your attributes.

00:37:54.700 --> 00:37:59.490
And we have also
announced access policies,

00:37:59.490 --> 00:38:01.990
which is a different
concept in cloud.

00:38:01.990 --> 00:38:06.540
But these access policies have
something called access levels.

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:11.910
These access levels can be
combined with IAM Conditions.

00:38:11.910 --> 00:38:17.220
So requests can be classified
with different access levels,

00:38:17.220 --> 00:38:22.230
again, depending on how safe
you think this access is.

00:38:22.230 --> 00:38:27.880
And then you put those in your
policies, in your bindings.

00:38:27.880 --> 00:38:30.580
So how would that looks like?

00:38:30.580 --> 00:38:33.240
Let's go back to one
of my earlier slides

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:38.040
with the you have a storage
admin, a powerful role.

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:40.830
And you have Mike
and admins again.

00:38:40.830 --> 00:38:44.880
But this time, I want to
restrict it to only work

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:46.950
for my corporate network.

00:38:46.950 --> 00:38:49.500
I would go and set
up access levels

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:52.290
that I define what that means.

00:38:52.290 --> 00:38:55.530
What does corporate
network mean to me?

00:38:55.530 --> 00:39:01.620
And now this will grant only
the storage admin this access

00:39:01.620 --> 00:39:03.603
if they meet that requirement.

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:10.790
So we have some key takeaways.

00:39:10.790 --> 00:39:17.570
We want to grant roles to
groups, not individual users,

00:39:17.570 --> 00:39:20.300
use the hierarchy.

00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:22.370
Think about where
in the hierarchy

00:39:22.370 --> 00:39:25.190
do I want to grant
this permission.

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:26.555
Is it a powerful one?

00:39:26.555 --> 00:39:28.580
Is it weaker one?

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:31.940
Use the correct role type
and the correct role,

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:33.620
for that matter.

00:39:33.620 --> 00:39:36.330
If you're doing a big
production system,

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:40.430
use the predefined role
or your custom roles.

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:44.320
Don't put any secrets
in your metadata.

00:39:44.320 --> 00:39:47.428
Turn on audit logging for
your sensitive workloads.

00:39:50.360 --> 00:39:52.700
Once you grow in
scale, start thinking

00:39:52.700 --> 00:39:57.860
about how to automate to
minimize the human errors.

00:39:57.860 --> 00:40:00.950
And lastly, bring your
organization structure

00:40:00.950 --> 00:40:02.330
to the cloud.

00:40:02.330 --> 00:40:05.540
And this means both in
terms of the hierarchy.

00:40:05.540 --> 00:40:07.630
It means how you create roles.

00:40:07.630 --> 00:40:09.920
How do you name your roles?

00:40:09.920 --> 00:40:13.790
Name them things that make sense
for you and your workloads.

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:15.180
How do you work in your company?

00:40:15.180 --> 00:40:17.990
Do you have a role that
you call data scientist?

00:40:17.990 --> 00:40:20.420
Maybe you create a role
named data scientist.

00:40:20.420 --> 00:40:24.140
Maybe have a group
for data scientists.

00:40:24.140 --> 00:40:28.700
Suddenly, it makes sense, and
it's much easier to manage.

00:40:28.700 --> 00:40:31.750
